<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sauerland</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sauerland is a hilly, wooded upland region in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-rhine-westphalia">North Rhine-Westphalia</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhr">Ruhr</a> valley. Much of it belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-of-arnsberg">County of Arnsberg</a> and from 1368 to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-westphalia">Duchy of Westphalia</a> ruled by the archbishops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, remaining Catholic. Long known for small-scale iron and metal working, it is now a popular area for hiking and winter sports, around Winterberg.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sauerland.jpg" alt="Sauerland" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jörg Braukmann · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kahler_Asten_(%C3%96senberg).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
